DM: For the past four years, he has gained a reputation as a pirate,

02:26:29.155 --> 02:26:39.175
DM: mostly preying on Tollander merchants passing south of here on the way to the distant trade routes to Ursk and faraway places to the east.

02:26:41.095 --> 02:26:41.975
DM: It is said that he

02:26:44.695 --> 02:26:46.415
DM: made a deal with a demon.

02:26:46.815 --> 02:26:46.995
Unknown: Hmm.

02:26:49.174 --> 02:26:52.175
DM: Acquired some sorcerous magic.

02:26:53.095 --> 02:26:58.575
DM: Hmm.

02:26:58.575 --> 02:26:58.895
DM: About

02:27:01.415 --> 02:27:03.155
DM: 10 months ago in the spring,

02:27:06.175 --> 02:27:08.195
DM: for reasons unbeknownst to us,

02:27:10.435 --> 02:27:10.635
DM: his

02:27:11.635 --> 02:27:15.675
DM: attention turned to the treasures of Vetta.

02:27:18.655 --> 02:27:23.295
DM: The sacred island of Vetta has had a temple of Kaikkea for

02:27:24.455 --> 02:27:25.915
DM: thousands of years.

02:27:29.135 --> 02:27:30.415
DM: It is also a place

02:27:31.875 --> 02:27:34.955
DM: where those not blessed by the goddess are forbidden to step,

02:27:36.955 --> 02:27:38.035
DM: and those who come

02:27:40.855 --> 02:27:42.955
DM: in deception or evil,

02:27:45.235 --> 02:27:45.355
DM: who

02:27:46.875 --> 02:27:48.735
DM: try to take the wealth of Vetta,

02:27:50.195 --> 02:27:53.415
DM: who try to leave in opposition to Kaikkea's will,

02:27:54.834 --> 02:27:55.635
DM: are cursed.

02:27:57.755 --> 02:27:59.775
DM: Their ships cannot sail out of port.

02:27:59.775 --> 02:28:01.475
DM: The water turns against them.

02:28:05.715 --> 02:28:05.815
Unknown: Hmm.

02:28:05.815 --> 02:28:10.315
DM: So few in our legends have ever attempted to steal from this place.

02:28:14.175 --> 02:28:15.355
DM: But Urgal landed, and

02:28:18.395 --> 02:28:24.015
DM: only one of the small community of priests and lay servants that lived there

02:28:25.075 --> 02:28:28.155
DM: managed to flee and return to us.

02:28:32.995 --> 02:28:34.155
DM: And he spoke of

02:28:35.235 --> 02:28:37.155
DM: slaughter and fire.

02:28:41.495 --> 02:28:42.935
DM: Several of our warriors

02:28:44.735 --> 02:28:48.635
DM: sailed to try and deal with this threat and never returned.

02:28:52.675 --> 02:28:57.195
DM: It's now been seven months since Urgal landed,

02:28:58.455 --> 02:29:00.475
DM: and nine of our best soldiers have

02:29:03.235 --> 02:29:03.635
DM: sought

02:29:05.355 --> 02:29:06.175
DM: to land and

02:29:08.515 --> 02:29:11.835
DM: purify the island without success.

02:29:11.835 --> 02:29:15.895
DM: [coughs] So a few months ago,

02:29:20.155 --> 02:29:21.435
DM: our district decided

02:29:22.795 --> 02:29:25.035
DM: we had no choice but to ask for outsiders,

02:29:27.155 --> 02:29:28.535
DM: and messages were sent to

02:29:29.975 --> 02:29:32.095
DM: Kaupa and Iskren and Tollen

02:29:33.235 --> 02:29:34.615
DM: to recruit adventurers.

02:29:40.115 --> 02:29:41.955
DM: And a group came.

02:29:41.975 --> 02:29:42.175
Unknown: Hmm.

02:29:44.215 --> 02:29:45.795
DM: Tollender mercenaries.

02:29:48.095 --> 02:29:49.215
DM: Kaikkea blessed them,

02:29:51.615 --> 02:29:54.415
DM: welcomed them on their journey, and they never returned.

02:29:57.695 --> 02:29:58.075
Seeker: How many?

02:29:59.875 --> 02:30:00.335
DM: Four.

02:30:03.094 --> 02:30:03.875
Seeker: Auspicious.

02:30:05.914 --> 02:30:12.115
Wellby: I say telepathically as a sidebar, "It's so nice that that wasn't Chardonian mercenaries."

02:30:12.375 --> 02:30:13.075
Wellby: Hmm.

02:30:13.115 --> 02:30:13.935
Wellby: It's just real nice.

02:30:13.955 --> 02:30:14.375
Kenzo: True.

02:30:15.475 --> 02:30:16.215
Wellby: Get used to that.

02:30:16.715 --> 02:30:18.195
DM: Skalme said you had-

02:30:18.935 --> 02:30:19.155
Unknown: Hmm

02:30:19.155 --> 02:30:19.155
DM: ...

02:30:21.395 --> 02:30:22.775
DM: significant power.

02:30:32.915 --> 02:30:33.775
Delwath: [coughs] Yes?

02:30:33.795 --> 02:30:36.695
DM: Would not force this mission on you after what I have said,

02:30:39.635 --> 02:30:40.615
DM: but we are desperate.

02:30:43.755 --> 02:30:44.155
Seeker: We,

02:30:48.395 --> 02:30:49.595
Delwath: uh, we have our own mission

02:30:53.235 --> 02:30:57.315
Delwath: for which we need access to the sacred waters of Vetta.

02:31:01.235 --> 02:31:02.195
DM: Then I hope you are

02:31:03.255 --> 02:31:04.815
DM: stronger and wiser than the

02:31:06.515 --> 02:31:08.195
Unknown: adventurers who have gone before.

02:31:09.095 --> 02:31:10.635
Kenzo: Do you know where the sacred waters are?

02:31:12.895 --> 02:31:16.055
DM: Oh, I've been on a pilgrimage several times, yes.

02:31:17.535 --> 02:31:19.135
Kenzo: Is there a map? Is there a, can you show us?

02:31:21.375 --> 02:31:25.835
DM: Yeah, I can draw a map. He, like, sits, he sketches a map, so he basically draws what you saw.

02:31:25.835 --> 02:31:26.375
Seeker: Mm-hmm.

02:31:26.435 --> 02:31:26.735
DM: The,

02:31:27.855 --> 02:31:37.895
DM: so the, on the side of the wall where there was, like, three large, low-covered buildings, there are hot springs underneath those buildings, and

02:31:37.895 --> 02:31:39.235
DM: those are the sacred waters.

02:31:39.335 --> 02:31:40.075
Delwath: Ah, okay. So

02:31:41.175 --> 02:31:42.895
Delwath: it goes dock-

02:31:42.995 --> 02:31:44.175
DM: Dock, road up

02:31:44.787 --> 02:31:45.587
Delwath: Entrance temple

02:31:45.587 --> 02:31:48.586
DM: ... entrance temple, like path through the caldera

02:31:48.586 --> 02:31:49.067
Kenzo: Wall

02:31:49.067 --> 02:31:49.127
DM: ... wall.

02:31:49.127 --> 02:31:49.667
Delwath: Okay

02:31:49.667 --> 02:31:52.647
DM: Then three large covered buildings.

02:31:52.747 --> 02:31:52.767
Unknown: Oh.

02:31:52.767 --> 02:31:57.827
DM: And underneath, inside those buildings are three different hot springs.

02:31:57.827 --> 02:32:02.667
DM: And he tells you the, the three springs, um, there's the Waters of Healing,

02:32:03.767 --> 02:32:07.467
DM: where people go to pray for restoration and healing.

02:32:07.967 --> 02:32:08.107
Unknown: Nice.

02:32:08.107 --> 02:32:14.767
DM: There's the Waters of Communion, where people go to, um, send messages and prayers to Kaikkea.

02:32:15.487 --> 02:32:22.887
DM: And then there's the s- the Sacred Waters themself, which are the source of the two other pools.

02:32:22.887 --> 02:32:23.127
Unknown: Nice.

02:32:23.147 --> 02:32:25.267
Kenzo: And so we fight the, uh, the lick down there.

02:32:26.407 --> 02:32:27.567
Wellby: The, well, the devil.

02:32:27.567 --> 02:32:28.227
Wellby: The devil.

02:32:28.947 --> 02:32:29.187
Unknown: Um,

02:32:31.067 --> 02:32:32.947
Unknown: do you know... Look, the, the...

02:32:33.347 --> 02:32:34.047
Seeker: What am I trying to say?

02:32:35.247 --> 02:32:36.887
Seeker: Why is the caldera divided in two?

02:32:39.047 --> 02:32:40.007
DM: This pilgrimage,

02:32:42.087 --> 02:32:42.787
DM: one must

02:32:44.447 --> 02:32:45.407
DM: walk through

02:32:47.047 --> 02:32:48.307
DM: Kaikkea's path.

02:32:49.166 --> 02:32:49.806
Seeker: A test.

02:32:51.307 --> 02:32:53.567
DM: Not a test, a spiritual journey.

02:33:02.427 --> 02:33:10.827
DM: A... It gives pilgrims time to focus on their prayers as they approach the holy waters themselves.

02:33:12.527 --> 02:33:14.747
Seeker: Is the ground hot there?

02:33:15.807 --> 02:33:17.567
DM: The ground is warm, yes.

02:33:19.327 --> 02:33:20.927
Seeker: And one must cross it barefoot?

02:33:22.147 --> 02:33:23.647
Unknown: No, it's not a test.

02:33:23.667 --> 02:33:24.647
Seeker: Hmm.

02:33:24.667 --> 02:33:26.527
DM: There is no... It is a,

02:33:29.427 --> 02:33:31.047
DM: it is a walking meditation.

02:33:33.267 --> 02:33:34.447
DM: It is a moment to

02:33:36.507 --> 02:33:38.927
DM: let Kaikkea's essence into your

02:33:40.467 --> 02:33:43.247
DM: soul as you prepare to ask her favor.

02:33:44.667 --> 02:33:45.607
Kenzo: As you prepare what?

02:33:45.607 --> 02:33:46.627
DM: Ask her favor.

02:33:57.727 --> 02:33:59.287
Wellby: What else should we know before we go there?

02:34:00.347 --> 02:34:01.047
Wellby: What should we be-

02:34:01.347 --> 02:34:04.687
Seeker: Well, we need to obtain... There's a matter of obtaining a blessing, I think.

02:34:04.687 --> 02:34:06.307
DM: Before you go, yes,

02:34:07.467 --> 02:34:08.187
DM: I will ask

02:34:09.087 --> 02:34:11.187
DM: for Kaikkea's blessing on your journey.

02:34:14.187 --> 02:34:14.307
Unknown: Nice.

02:34:14.307 --> 02:34:15.087
DM: And if you wish,

02:34:17.647 --> 02:34:18.707
DM: you should speak with

02:34:19.827 --> 02:34:20.467
DM: Tulvak.

02:34:21.427 --> 02:34:25.487
DM: He's the ferry captain who made it back alive after-

02:34:27.747 --> 02:34:28.027
Wellby: Yes,

02:34:29.267 --> 02:34:29.367
Unknown: the-

02:34:29.367 --> 02:34:30.147
DM: ... Urgall's attack.

02:34:32.227 --> 02:34:35.687
Delwath: The ship that's docked there now, is that Urgall's?

02:34:36.927 --> 02:34:38.187
DM: I believe so, yes.

02:34:39.107 --> 02:34:43.987
DM: There is mists and fogs that surround the island, and you're not able to see anything.

02:34:43.987 --> 02:34:46.467
DM: All who venture through the mists have not returned.

02:34:47.907 --> 02:34:51.147
DM: Tulvak did speak of a pirate ship docking and

02:34:52.727 --> 02:34:54.067
DM: 50 pirates or so

02:34:56.327 --> 02:34:59.747
DM: disembarking with flaming swords.

02:35:01.327 --> 02:35:03.387
Seeker: 50 pirates with flaming swords?

02:35:04.987 --> 02:35:05.207
Wellby: Hmm.

02:35:07.247 --> 02:35:11.447
Unknown: Is there anyone who knew Ur- Ur- Urle, Urle was-

02:35:11.507 --> 02:35:11.967
DM: Urgall

02:35:11.967 --> 02:35:15.327
Delwath: ... Urgall?

02:35:15.327 --> 02:35:16.007
DM: Like I say,

02:35:18.187 --> 02:35:20.207
DM: he was not from these part of the islands.

02:35:25.007 --> 02:35:25.827
DM: I don't know who...

02:35:27.767 --> 02:35:28.867
DM: He was from the west.

02:35:38.587 --> 02:35:40.047
DM: I'm sure there are those who knew him

02:35:41.327 --> 02:35:43.627
DM: in the western part of our archipelago.

02:35:45.667 --> 02:35:46.047
DM: I could

02:35:48.607 --> 02:35:51.307
DM: give you a token of introduction should you wish to

02:35:52.247 --> 02:35:53.187
DM: travel that way.

02:35:57.787 --> 02:35:59.207
Seeker: There's something missing from the story.

02:36:00.727 --> 02:36:04.867
Seeker: He was a privateer of some kind, so thought to be stealing wealth from

02:36:06.287 --> 02:36:10.707
Seeker: traders or from these islands in some way, then he arrived on this one,

02:36:12.167 --> 02:36:14.387
Seeker: triggering the curse, and then didn't leave again.

02:36:16.367 --> 02:36:19.507
Wellby: Something made him turn back towards these islands?

02:36:22.047 --> 02:36:23.447
Seeker: Or did he leave? Is he still on there?

02:36:25.287 --> 02:36:25.527
DM: I mean,

02:36:26.787 --> 02:36:28.427
DM: nobody knows. Presumably,

02:36:31.167 --> 02:36:33.487
DM: he hasn't, like, sailed off on a ship, right?

02:36:34.147 --> 02:36:35.987
Seeker: But the curse would prevent him.

02:36:35.987 --> 02:36:38.007
DM: Theoretically, the curse would prevent him, yes.

02:36:40.587 --> 02:36:43.847
Seeker: So telepathically, should we talk about what we saw from the air,

02:36:45.307 --> 02:36:47.287
Seeker: the creatures, whatever those were?

02:36:48.347 --> 02:36:48.507
Wellby: Yeah.

02:36:49.567 --> 02:36:50.947
Wellby: We can put it all out there, I think.

02:36:51.747 --> 02:36:54.307
Unknown: I think we should talk to, uh, the guy who-

02:36:54.367 --> 02:36:55.367
Wellby: Yeah, the guy that came back

02:36:55.367 --> 02:36:56.407
Delwath: ... who came back.

02:36:56.447 --> 02:36:56.507
Wellby: Yeah.

02:36:56.587 --> 02:36:57.087
Seeker: We, uh,

02:36:59.447 --> 02:37:05.447
Seeker: we passed over by air and saw some demonic beasts on the steps of the temple,

02:37:06.747 --> 02:37:10.447
Seeker: milling about, unclear who might be in charge.

02:37:13.607 --> 02:37:14.747
DM: Well, Urgall was...

02:37:18.387 --> 02:37:20.187
Unknown: Rumor has it that he made...

02:37:21.447 --> 02:37:23.547
DM: He had some fiendish connection.

02:37:25.307 --> 02:37:25.527
Unknown: Oh, yeah?

02:37:25.567 --> 02:37:26.527
DM: He was a sorcerer

02:37:27.407 --> 02:37:30.107
DM: or warlock of a demonic power.

02:37:33.527 --> 02:37:36.927
Seeker: Would these things with the flaming swords have had skull faces and horns?

02:37:39.087 --> 02:37:41.367
DM: You would have to talk to Tulvak.

02:37:42.007 --> 02:37:42.687
Seeker: I think we should.

02:37:43.207 --> 02:37:43.447
Wellby: Yeah.

02:37:47.363 --> 02:37:50.143
DM: I would actually suggest you not depart until the morning

02:37:50.823 --> 02:37:51.903
Kenzo: I was gonna suggest the same

02:37:51.943 --> 02:37:52.063
Wellby: Mm.

02:37:52.063 --> 02:37:53.243
Unknown: [clears throat]

02:37:53.303 --> 02:37:55.443
Unknown: Mm-hmm. That seems reasonable.

02:37:58.363 --> 02:38:01.643
DM: I will be ready for the blessings at dawn,

02:38:03.422 --> 02:38:04.883
DM: if that is acceptable to you.

02:38:05.323 --> 02:38:05.443
Wellby: Yeah.

02:38:05.443 --> 02:38:07.223
DM: You're welcome to spend the night here.

02:38:09.543 --> 02:38:11.303
Unknown: Could we talk to Tulvak this evening?

02:38:12.243 --> 02:38:13.023
DM: Yes, I will

02:38:13.783 --> 02:38:15.323
DM: send a messenger to fetch him.

02:38:19.123 --> 02:38:20.423
DM: Is there anything else I can do?

02:38:22.243 --> 02:38:23.763
Unknown: Is there anything you need?

02:38:30.083 --> 02:38:33.923
Unknown: Is there anything you need?

02:38:33.923 --> 02:38:34.443
Delwath: Don't think so.

02:38:35.183 --> 02:38:37.843
Wellby: Warm place to sleep for the night wouldn't go bad.

02:38:39.643 --> 02:38:43.003
DM: The fire always burns in the church, and you've got plenty of furs.

02:38:43.463 --> 02:38:43.603
Wellby: Mm.

02:38:44.743 --> 02:38:45.523
DM: That will be warm.

02:38:50.763 --> 02:38:52.283
Unknown: I will, I will, uh,

02:38:55.083 --> 02:38:58.983
DM: leave you to your food and your conversation, and I will send

02:39:00.103 --> 02:39:01.183
DM: Tulvak when he is

02:39:02.703 --> 02:39:07.243
Delwath: ready to speak.

02:39:07.243 --> 02:39:08.003
Kenzo: So what's the plan?

02:39:09.483 --> 02:39:09.903
Wellby: Mm.

02:39:11.243 --> 02:39:13.563
Wellby: Speak with Tulvak, get blessed.

02:39:17.363 --> 02:39:17.743
Delwath: Yeah.

02:39:17.743 --> 02:39:18.823
Wellby: Drop in vertically-

02:39:18.823 --> 02:39:18.943
Wellby: Fly over

02:39:18.943 --> 02:39:20.643
Unknown: ... directly to the location of the waters

02:39:20.703 --> 02:39:23.723
Delwath: I was gonna say, yeah, why would we not just go straight to the waters?

02:39:25.263 --> 02:39:27.483
Wellby: Because it's a pilgrimage.

02:39:28.383 --> 02:39:30.103
Wellby: I will be saying this hesitant...

02:39:30.103 --> 02:39:31.083
Wellby: I mean, without knowing, right?

02:39:31.483 --> 02:39:32.603
Seeker: Could be disrespectful-

02:39:32.623 --> 02:39:33.223
Wellby: Because it's a pilgrimage, right?

02:39:33.223 --> 02:39:35.623
Seeker: ... to bypass the, the...

02:39:35.723 --> 02:39:36.143
Delwath: Um.

02:39:36.503 --> 02:39:37.083
Kenzo: It's a ritual.

02:39:37.583 --> 02:39:38.303
Wellby: It could be.

02:39:38.603 --> 02:39:39.263
Kenzo: Well, and also the, the-

02:39:39.283 --> 02:39:39.863
Delwath: You could ask

02:39:39.863 --> 02:39:42.043
Kenzo: ... don't we need the, uh... Don't we need the, um,

02:39:43.463 --> 02:39:45.323
Kenzo: the scepter submerged there for 24 hours?

02:39:45.923 --> 02:39:47.043
Seeker: We do. Oh, yeah, that's true.

02:39:48.723 --> 02:39:51.723
Wellby: Which probably means we need to clear or hold this.

02:39:53.723 --> 02:39:53.843
Unknown: Yeah.

02:39:53.863 --> 02:39:57.923
Wellby: I- it's probably not good enough just to submerge the s- the scepter for 24 hours and leave,

02:39:59.243 --> 02:40:02.943
Wellby: without taking care of the pirate and the demons and the...

02:40:03.243 --> 02:40:05.743
Delwath: I just wonder if the pirate or the demon

02:40:06.803 --> 02:40:10.283
Delwath: would be... [coughs] The waters will either still be...

02:40:12.343 --> 02:40:15.263
Delwath: They're either gonna be a safe spot or the heart of the problem, right?

02:40:15.723 --> 02:40:16.103
Kenzo: I think.

02:40:16.103 --> 02:40:16.443
Wellby: Agreed.

02:40:16.443 --> 02:40:19.783
Seeker: We really don't know. Also, it's the relationship between the demons we saw and the pirate.

02:40:19.783 --> 02:40:20.563
Wellby: Maybe the pirate's, like-

02:40:20.923 --> 02:40:21.343
Wellby: Yeah

02:40:21.343 --> 02:40:23.983
Seeker: ... held captive. Maybe the pirate is leading those demons.

02:40:24.103 --> 02:40:26.543
Wellby: We saw the demon on the outside of the volcano.

02:40:26.543 --> 02:40:28.403
Unknown: Maybe they can't get in. I...

02:40:28.403 --> 02:40:28.723
Kenzo: Who knows?

02:40:28.763 --> 02:40:29.263
Wellby: It could be.

02:40:29.943 --> 02:40:30.083
Unknown: But if they-

02:40:30.103 --> 02:40:31.523
Wellby: Right, they were milling around on the steps.

02:40:31.543 --> 02:40:31.563
Unknown: Yeah.

02:40:31.563 --> 02:40:32.383
Wellby: And there were a bunch of them, right?

02:40:32.743 --> 02:40:32.983
Unknown: Yeah.

02:40:33.023 --> 02:40:34.143
DM: There was, like, 10.

02:40:34.143 --> 02:40:34.823
Wellby: And there were flying o-

02:40:34.823 --> 02:40:34.963
Unknown: But-

02:40:34.963 --> 02:40:35.303
Wellby: ... little flying ones

02:40:35.303 --> 02:40:35.883
DM: ... starting on the inside-

02:40:35.883 --> 02:40:36.423
Delwath: Little flying ones and-

02:40:36.423 --> 02:40:37.723
Delwath: ... and working out feels-

02:40:38.063 --> 02:40:38.423
Delwath: Bigger

02:40:38.423 --> 02:40:39.623
Delwath: ... safer,

02:40:41.743 --> 02:40:42.183
Wellby: I think.

02:40:43.843 --> 02:40:44.783
Wellby: Well, let's, let's talk to this guy.

02:40:44.803 --> 02:40:45.863
Delwath: Let's, let's talk to this guy.

02:40:47.523 --> 02:40:56.163
Wellby: Yeah. Uh, as we pull around the fire, I think I'll, uh, ask, "Seeker, I've heard stories that I never knew if they were true or not, that in the deeps of

02:40:57.743 --> 02:41:01.223
Wellby: the earth there are fiery places, that there are demons in the deeps.

02:41:01.223 --> 02:41:02.003
Wellby: Is that true?"

02:41:03.303 --> 02:41:05.043
Seeker: Demons in the deeps of the earth?

02:41:07.083 --> 02:41:08.963
Seeker: Uh, you'd think I would know.

02:41:08.963 --> 02:41:11.243
Unknown: Tim, what do I know? [laughs]

02:41:11.343 --> 02:41:11.903
DM: Ah.

02:41:11.903 --> 02:41:12.683
Wellby: Make it up.

02:41:12.743 --> 02:41:13.923
DM: Yeah, just make something up.

02:41:15.963 --> 02:41:17.243
DM: Are there demons in the deeps?

02:41:17.243 --> 02:41:21.563
DM: [clears throat] There could be.

02:41:21.563 --> 02:41:22.623
DM: There doesn't have to be.

02:41:25.243 --> 02:41:28.203
Seeker: One thing that the dwarves have learned as you descend through

02:41:29.683 --> 02:41:31.203
Seeker: the crust, as we call it,

02:41:32.383 --> 02:41:33.743
Seeker: is that it warms up as you go.

02:41:34.443 --> 02:41:34.983
Wellby: Oh.

02:41:34.983 --> 02:41:36.583
Seeker: The minerals become,

02:41:37.723 --> 02:41:40.703
Seeker: uh, more uniform and s- hard.

02:41:42.223 --> 02:41:46.023
Seeker: Uh, but there are also, uh, s- uh, strange...

02:41:46.923 --> 02:41:47.863
Seeker: Oh, what's the word for that?

02:41:49.723 --> 02:41:51.843
Seeker: Bubbles and other exclusions-

02:41:52.303 --> 02:41:53.063
Wellby: Mm

02:41:53.063 --> 02:41:55.703
Seeker: ... uh, from the stone that you suddenly penetrate into, and

02:41:58.943 --> 02:42:02.723
Seeker: those are interesting to explore, but we don't always hear back from the people that try to

02:42:03.942 --> 02:42:04.963
Seeker: go deep into them.

02:42:07.503 --> 02:42:07.743
Unknown: Uh...

02:42:09.643 --> 02:42:11.163
Wellby: What's the deepest you've ever gone?

02:42:11.583 --> 02:42:14.143
Wellby: Like, have you been as far below the earth as we've flown above it?

02:42:15.423 --> 02:42:17.403
Unknown: There's one that's used, uh,

02:42:18.283 --> 02:42:28.203
Seeker: not exactly as a tourist attraction, 'cause people don't, you know, travel high up into the mountains at great cost and discomfort to visit this thing, but for curiosity seekers,

02:42:29.323 --> 02:42:31.223
Seeker: uh, there is the occasional visit.

02:42:31.223 --> 02:42:33.023
Seeker: It's a, um... If you imagine a,

02:42:34.463 --> 02:42:39.423
Seeker: a spherical space large enough to easily park our ship, but it is, uh,

02:42:40.343 --> 02:42:41.523
Seeker: f- full halfway to the top,

02:42:42.723 --> 02:42:47.283
Seeker: uh, with what we believe to be mo- a liquid mostly composed of, uh,

02:42:49.003 --> 02:42:51.523
Seeker: uh, uh, quicksilver.

02:42:52.943 --> 02:42:53.983
Seeker: Uh, and it's very interesting.

02:42:55.363 --> 02:43:01.663
Seeker: Actually, I recall now that I went on a school trip because it was a place to, to demonstrate density concepts.

02:43:02.903 --> 02:43:03.303
Seeker: Uh, you could-

02:43:03.343 --> 02:43:03.363
Wellby: Mm

02:43:03.363 --> 02:43:06.123
Seeker: ... for example, float a solid bar of gold in it.

02:43:06.923 --> 02:43:11.543
Seeker: You had to be careful not to drop it in, though, because it would sink and then only very slowly rise back again to the surface.

02:43:12.243 --> 02:43:14.103
Seeker: They cautioned us not to swim in it for some reason.

02:43:14.663 --> 02:43:17.223
Wellby: When you went on a field trip, your teacher brought a solid bar of gold?

02:43:18.743 --> 02:43:19.503
Wellby: That's amazing.

02:43:19.503 --> 02:43:23.663
Unknown: A wealthy school. Uh [laughs].

02:43:23.803 --> 02:43:24.543
Wellby: That's so cool.

02:43:25.563 --> 02:43:25.843
DM: Uh...

02:43:27.323 --> 02:43:28.503
Wellby: Are there things that live in it?

02:43:30.583 --> 02:43:31.563
Seeker: Not that we've encountered.

02:43:34.423 --> 02:43:39.603
Seeker: The s- It was unpleasant to breathe in that chamber, as I recall, so you couldn't stay in there too long.

02:43:39.723 --> 02:43:42.223
Unknown: Is this far underground or above...

02:43:42.223 --> 02:43:42.863
Delwath: Far deep?

02:43:42.923 --> 02:43:44.003
Seeker: Quite, quite deep, yes.

02:43:44.543 --> 02:43:45.483
Delwath: Mm-hmm. The, um...

02:43:49.303 --> 02:43:51.523
Delwath: My unit in the Great War,

02:43:52.923 --> 02:43:53.043
Unknown: the-

02:43:55.595 --> 02:44:00.255
Delwath: One of the most talented warriors, she, uh, she told a story,

02:44:02.535 --> 02:44:03.635
Delwath: I never know if it was true,

02:44:05.155 --> 02:44:11.835
Delwath: of going deep beneath the Earth to hunt fiends with some dwarves she said.

02:44:12.175 --> 02:44:12.375
Unknown: Hmm.

02:44:13.275 --> 02:44:17.895
Delwath: They were the, the scourge of the West in, um, during the Great War, these great

02:44:19.315 --> 02:44:21.035
Delwath: beasts, fiendish beasts,

02:44:22.895 --> 02:44:28.255
Delwath: 30, 40 feet tall with flaming whips burning the forest behind them.

02:44:29.575 --> 02:44:31.635
Seeker: We never saw anything like that on a field trip.

02:44:31.635 --> 02:44:33.915
Delwath: [laughs] Ah.

02:44:33.915 --> 02:44:35.015
Delwath: I think they came from

02:44:36.295 --> 02:44:37.915
Delwath: Shemhad. Uh, but-

02:44:37.955 --> 02:44:41.775
Wellby: Are there roads underground then that the dwarves didn't make?

02:44:43.895 --> 02:44:44.755
Delwath: That's what she said.

02:44:45.435 --> 02:44:46.395
Seeker: That's definitely the case.

02:44:47.535 --> 02:44:48.835
Delwath: She said they would go...

02:44:48.835 --> 02:44:50.755
Delwath: She went deep beneath the Earth.

02:44:51.495 --> 02:44:53.675
Delwath: She was many generations older than me.

02:44:53.675 --> 02:44:54.735
Delwath: This must have been

02:44:56.255 --> 02:44:58.135
Unknown: in the age of Drancor if not before.

02:45:00.235 --> 02:45:00.515
DM: Uh,

02:45:02.035 --> 02:45:03.655
Unknown: but there was a, a...

02:45:03.655 --> 02:45:04.955
Delwath: She was part of a group that would

02:45:06.495 --> 02:45:10.575
Delwath: go beneath the dwarven cities, uh, down for many days

02:45:11.775 --> 02:45:13.135
Delwath: to the roots of the mountains

02:45:14.235 --> 02:45:15.395
Delwath: and walk amongst them

02:45:16.735 --> 02:45:17.375
Delwath: and hunt.

02:45:19.835 --> 02:45:20.375
Seeker: Wait, what?

02:45:21.995 --> 02:45:22.755
Seeker: Hunt these fiends.

02:45:22.755 --> 02:45:23.215
Delwath: Fiends.

02:45:23.755 --> 02:45:27.535
Delwath: Hmm.

02:45:27.535 --> 02:45:30.395
Wellby: What do the dwarves say is underneath the roots of the mountain?

02:45:32.975 --> 02:45:34.395
Wellby: Is it mountains all the way down?

02:45:36.475 --> 02:45:46.215
Seeker: This fae we just spoke with mentioned something that I had heard rumor of, which is that there are portals to the Plane of Stone

02:45:47.735 --> 02:45:51.175
Seeker: beneath the roots of the mountains, very deep.

02:45:51.175 --> 02:45:52.255
Seeker: I never personally have been

02:45:53.795 --> 02:45:56.535
Seeker: down that far because obviously there's

02:45:57.635 --> 02:45:59.015
Seeker: dangerous creatures down there, too.

02:45:59.015 --> 02:45:59.575
Seeker: It's not all

02:46:01.295 --> 02:46:02.755
Seeker: dwarvish transit.

02:46:03.655 --> 02:46:03.775
Unknown: Hmm.

02:46:08.235 --> 02:46:10.495
Seeker: What are these portals to the Plane of Stone like?

02:46:10.495 --> 02:46:10.775
Kenzo: I don't

02:46:12.735 --> 02:46:12.795
Unknown: know.

02:46:12.795 --> 02:46:16.195
Seeker: What would the Plane of El- Element of Stone be like?

02:46:16.195 --> 02:46:20.795
DM: I mean, it's like you can't really move through it unless you can burn.

02:46:20.795 --> 02:46:22.895
Unknown: It's all solid and it's not...

02:46:23.775 --> 02:46:23.775
Unknown: Hmm.

02:46:23.875 --> 02:46:24.595
Wellby: I wonder-

02:46:24.675 --> 02:46:26.115
DM: I mean, there's pockets of air.

02:46:26.115 --> 02:46:29.215
DM: There's like pockets of gems and things, but there just seems-

02:46:29.235 --> 02:46:31.115
Unknown: There just seems between,

02:46:32.275 --> 02:46:34.875
Seeker: uh, materials and temperatures, gradients

02:46:37.335 --> 02:46:38.895
Seeker: you can transit between them.

02:46:42.955 --> 02:46:47.135
Wellby: Would you know... Like, if you were a dwarf and you dug deep enough, would you know

02:46:48.715 --> 02:46:51.155
Wellby: when you left this... I wonder this about the Eastern Ocean, too.

02:46:52.175 --> 02:46:55.155
Delwath: Would you know when you left, uh, Taelgar and ended on the Plane of Stone?

02:46:55.595 --> 02:46:56.095
Wellby: Right.

02:46:56.255 --> 02:46:57.275
Delwath: Well, I wonder. Would you?

02:46:59.335 --> 02:47:01.775
Seeker: I would guess that I would know because

02:47:03.175 --> 02:47:05.715
Seeker: someone less versed in geology might not.

02:47:05.715 --> 02:47:09.075
Seeker: There are strange phenomena down there at very high temperatures and pressures of course.

02:47:09.095 --> 02:47:09.975
Seeker: Mm-hmm.

02:47:09.975 --> 02:47:17.655
Seeker: But you would transition presumably to one where the world is made of a different material than you'd expect here on Taelgar or

02:47:18.735 --> 02:47:20.395
Seeker: the way things moved or changed.

02:47:22.375 --> 02:47:25.635
Seeker: What would stone be like given life and agency?

02:47:25.635 --> 02:47:28.035
Seeker: Presumably it's all alive there in the Plane of Stone.

02:47:28.035 --> 02:47:28.415
Kenzo: Oh, no.

02:47:29.735 --> 02:47:31.395
Unknown: Kenzo's drifted off to sleep.

02:47:32.035 --> 02:47:32.295
Wellby: Hmm.

02:47:34.935 --> 02:47:38.335
Kenzo: Kenzo's, uh, bridging the land of the dreamworld.

02:47:38.335 --> 02:47:39.535
Seeker: Between worlds, yeah.

02:47:40.655 --> 02:47:40.775
Unknown: Hmm.

02:47:41.935 --> 02:47:42.355
Seeker: I'm here.

02:47:43.535 --> 02:47:44.035
Wellby: You're good.

02:47:44.735 --> 02:47:46.775
Unknown: Ah, it's a good time for Kenzo to take a little nap.

02:47:48.275 --> 02:47:49.275
Delwath: Yeah, it was the evening.

02:47:50.315 --> 02:47:54.395
Delwath: Yeah. Kenzo's like e- eating too much fried fish.

02:47:55.795 --> 02:47:58.635
Unknown: Does, does food remind you of your, uh, childhood?

02:47:58.635 --> 02:48:00.175
Unknown: Did you eat, eat fish a lot?

02:48:01.855 --> 02:48:02.395
Wellby: Yeah. We

02:48:03.475 --> 02:48:04.795
Wellby: fished constantly.

02:48:06.495 --> 02:48:11.535
Wellby: I, uh, you know, there were always lines going off the side of the boat when we were going across.

02:48:11.535 --> 02:48:14.774
Wellby: It's the same sort of fish here, though they prepare it a lot more simply than we did.

02:48:17.655 --> 02:48:18.035
Wellby: We had...

02:48:19.095 --> 02:48:20.735
Wellby: I guess it would be like a quartermaster.

02:48:20.735 --> 02:48:22.675
Wellby: I mean, people on the ship whose whole job was just to

02:48:24.535 --> 02:48:26.215
Wellby: cook and covering the ground we did.

02:48:26.215 --> 02:48:27.155
Wellby: It was, uh,

02:48:28.015 --> 02:48:30.915
Unknown: halfling food. It's so good.

02:48:32.455 --> 02:48:33.655
Wellby: Not like this human stuff.

02:48:34.275 --> 02:48:35.515
DM: Hmm.

02:48:35.515 --> 02:48:36.755
Delwath: It's true. The humans are, um,

02:48:39.615 --> 02:48:40.155
Delwath: simplistic

02:48:43.155 --> 02:48:44.535
Delwath: in their cooking.

02:48:45.055 --> 02:48:45.895
DM: The elves are-

02:48:45.915 --> 02:48:46.575
Kenzo: Very often

02:48:46.575 --> 02:48:50.475
Kenzo: ... the elves are condescending in their boldness.

02:48:50.475 --> 02:48:53.035
Delwath: Wellby was the one who said it was better than human food.

02:48:53.035 --> 02:48:56.415
Unknown: [laughs]

02:48:56.415 --> 02:48:56.595
DM: [laughs]

02:48:56.595 --> 02:48:57.115
DM: I think, uh,

02:48:58.835 --> 02:49:00.675
Delwath: human food is a bit of an acquired taste.

02:49:01.875 --> 02:49:07.775
Wellby: I think so. For the halflings though, there would be all these traditional dishes that we'd make.

02:49:07.775 --> 02:49:16.375
Wellby: Like, every other day was a traditional day of some kind or another, like somebody's porridge from 1,000 years ago that we always eat on the-

02:49:16.375 --> 02:49:17.335
Kenzo: 1,000-year-old porridge?

02:49:17.335 --> 02:49:19.315
Wellby: Well, the recipe was 1,000 years old.

02:49:19.335 --> 02:49:19.375
Unknown: Oh, okay.

02:49:19.375 --> 02:49:21.655
Wellby: It tasted like it was 1,000 years old.

02:49:21.695 --> 02:49:22.295
Delwath: Oh, wow.

02:49:28.355 --> 02:49:28.415
Unknown: Hmm.

02:49:28.455 --> 02:49:29.675
Wellby: All right. I'll turn in, too.

02:49:31.675 --> 02:49:32.735
DM: So-

02:49:32.755 --> 02:49:33.875
DM: Wait. Go ahead. You haven't said something.

02:49:33.875 --> 02:49:35.195
Kenzo: Sorry. I may have missed it.

02:49:35.215 --> 02:49:35.915
Kenzo: Did we talk to that guy?

02:49:36.395 --> 02:49:37.635
DM: He's about to show up.

02:49:37.635 --> 02:49:37.995
Delwath: Oh, nice.

02:49:37.995 --> 02:49:38.275
DM: Cool.

02:49:39.355 --> 02:49:44.515
DM: So as you are finishing talking about food, um, you see,

02:49:45.895 --> 02:49:47.695
DM: you see walking up, um, a,

02:49:50.475 --> 02:49:57.915
DM: a, a man also dressed heavily in furs as is, um- As is, you know, the sort of

02:49:59.035 --> 02:50:02.135
DM: standard around here, um, dressed in, um,

02:50:03.355 --> 02:50:10.915
DM: like furs but also like a thick sh- like sealskin jacket against the wind and the rain.

02:50:11.435 --> 02:50:12.895
DM: Um, not, he's not a priest.

02:50:12.895 --> 02:50:14.895
DM: He's not tattooed. Uh,

02:50:16.515 --> 02:50:19.535
DM: he, he sort of approaches a bit, a bit tentatively,

02:50:21.235 --> 02:50:25.015
DM: kind of looking at all these, like, you know,

02:50:26.075 --> 02:50:27.575
DM: he's probably never seen an elf before.

02:50:27.575 --> 02:50:31.855
Unknown: [laughs]

02:50:31.875 --> 02:50:32.755
DM: Um, Vaallinen said, "You

02:50:33.815 --> 02:50:34.515
DM: wish to speak?"

02:50:36.515 --> 02:50:36.915
DM: He sort of

02:50:38.075 --> 02:50:40.095
DM: edges his way towards the fire.

02:50:40.095 --> 02:50:41.515
DM: "May, may I sit?"

02:50:42.315 --> 02:50:42.615
Delwath: Yes.

02:50:42.995 --> 02:50:43.735
Seeker: Please.

02:50:43.755 --> 02:50:44.155
Delwath: Please.

02:50:47.755 --> 02:50:48.015
DM: "Can I,

02:50:49.195 --> 02:50:49.595
DM: what can I...

02:50:50.835 --> 02:50:52.095
DM: Can I help you?

02:50:52.095 --> 02:50:53.655
Unknown: How can I, what can I do?"

02:50:53.655 --> 02:50:54.655
Delwath: Tell us your story.

02:50:56.155 --> 02:50:58.215
DM: He sort of shudders slightly a bit.

02:50:58.215 --> 02:51:03.395
DM: "It's not, it's not a good story."

02:51:05.575 --> 02:51:09.075
Kenzo: We are heading there in the morning, and anything you can remember

02:51:10.175 --> 02:51:12.135
Delwath: may help us succeed at restoring it,

02:51:13.015 --> 02:51:14.255
Delwath: even the smallest detail.

02:51:15.635 --> 02:51:17.455
Unknown: I know it's hard to recall, but

02:51:18.515 --> 02:51:21.155
Delwath: whatever you can would be very helpful.

02:51:21.155 --> 02:51:23.175
DM: "It was night.

02:51:25.755 --> 02:51:26.015
DM: Weren't e-

02:51:27.135 --> 02:51:28.395
DM: weren't expecting anything.

02:51:30.275 --> 02:51:30.575
DM: I had...

02:51:31.875 --> 02:51:33.475
DM: Don't, don't usually stay over.

02:51:33.475 --> 02:51:34.035
DM: I often...

02:51:35.315 --> 02:51:35.375
DM: I,

02:51:36.595 --> 02:51:38.555
DM: I, I used to sail back, back and forth.

02:51:40.515 --> 02:51:43.375
DM: I brought food, brought supplies, brought wood,

02:51:44.555 --> 02:51:45.475
DM: took pilgrims.

02:51:49.255 --> 02:51:50.855
DM: It was, it was the spring,

02:51:52.915 --> 02:51:53.155
DM: and

02:51:55.175 --> 02:51:55.655
DM: cold,

02:51:57.155 --> 02:51:58.035
DM: and I decided to

02:51:59.295 --> 02:52:06.715
DM: just sleep. I, I thought, 'Mm, I'll, I'll just spend the night in the longhouse, the pilgrims' longhouse at the docks.'

02:52:08.115 --> 02:52:08.335
DM: And

02:52:10.095 --> 02:52:11.735
DM: the island is

02:52:12.935 --> 02:52:16.935
DM: safe. It's never been attacked.

02:52:16.935 --> 02:52:17.295
DM: How could...

02:52:18.995 --> 02:52:22.135
DM: I think now I should have gone, but how could, how could I have known?"

02:52:24.755 --> 02:52:27.975
DM: He looks around nervously. "The, the fire is what I remember.

02:52:30.595 --> 02:52:31.675
DM: The ship docked, and

02:52:34.155 --> 02:52:34.755
DM: a great

02:52:35.815 --> 02:52:37.575
DM: explosion of flame,

02:52:39.255 --> 02:52:42.575
DM: burning flame that cracked open in a flash of

02:52:44.475 --> 02:52:44.655
DM: the

02:52:45.855 --> 02:52:46.835
DM: pilgrims. I had...

02:52:48.315 --> 02:52:50.975
DM: I think the only reason I survived was,

02:52:52.895 --> 02:52:54.715
DM: uh, maybe it was Kaikkea's doing.

02:52:54.735 --> 02:52:54.975
DM: I had,

02:52:56.295 --> 02:53:00.975
DM: I had woken up to relieve myself in the night when I shone the, saw the ship,

02:53:02.535 --> 02:53:03.235
DM: and I ran

02:53:04.295 --> 02:53:06.695
DM: as the fires consumed the longhouse,

02:53:08.155 --> 02:53:09.355
DM: cracking and burning."

02:53:10.715 --> 02:53:11.995
Seeker: You didn't see what started the fire?

02:53:11.995 --> 02:53:17.075
DM: "It came from one of the, the captain's hand."

02:53:17.075 --> 02:53:17.195
Unknown: Hmm.

02:53:18.615 --> 02:53:19.235
DM: Cast a

02:53:21.555 --> 02:53:22.095
DM: flaming

02:53:25.115 --> 02:53:26.995
DM: ball of fire from his hands.

02:53:27.715 --> 02:53:28.775
Delwath: Hmm, I see.

02:53:28.775 --> 02:53:29.215
Seeker: Hmm.

02:53:29.215 --> 02:53:29.695
Delwath: Fireball.

02:53:30.755 --> 02:53:31.455
Delwath: Sorcerer.

02:53:33.615 --> 02:53:35.195
DM: And then his men

02:53:36.375 --> 02:53:39.495
DM: stormed off the ship, killing everybody who ran out of the building.

02:53:43.015 --> 02:53:43.915
DM: I was hiding.

02:53:46.735 --> 02:53:51.435
DM: And once everybody was dead, I could hear the laughs and smell the burning flesh, and

02:53:53.555 --> 02:53:56.355
DM: I don't really remember the next few hours.

02:53:58.295 --> 02:53:59.235
Wellby: How did you get away?

02:54:01.475 --> 02:54:02.915
DM: I don't really remember. I was...

02:54:04.595 --> 02:54:06.255
DM: Sort of came to my senses

02:54:07.355 --> 02:54:08.175
DM: in a rowboat

02:54:09.735 --> 02:54:12.855
DM: a mile offshore. I must have ran in terror or

02:54:16.154 --> 02:54:17.575
DM: did something once they had...

02:54:21.315 --> 02:54:22.115
DM: I just remember the

02:54:23.515 --> 02:54:28.335
DM: thud of their boots and the fires that flickered from their swords as they-

02:54:30.155 --> 02:54:30.595
Seeker: Did you...

02:54:31.955 --> 02:54:37.815
Delwath: The one who cast the, who ignited the initial fire, did you get a good look at him?

02:54:38.735 --> 02:54:39.395
DM: It was dark.

02:54:40.215 --> 02:54:41.295
DM: He seemed to be the leader.

02:54:42.735 --> 02:54:43.875
Delwath: Was he human?

02:54:47.455 --> 02:54:48.295
DM: He wore this

02:54:49.655 --> 02:54:50.535
DM: helmet with

02:54:53.435 --> 02:54:55.495
DM: demon horns on it,

02:54:57.215 --> 02:55:00.115
DM: but from what I could see of his face, he seemed human enough.

02:55:00.955 --> 02:55:02.735
Unknown: Anyone with a skull face?

02:55:06.135 --> 02:55:07.315
DM: A skull face?

02:55:08.635 --> 02:55:09.635
Seeker: Something not human.

02:55:10.955 --> 02:55:12.295
Delwath: Creatures from a nightmare.

02:55:13.475 --> 02:55:15.015
Unknown: I didn't, I didn't look.

02:55:16.155 --> 02:55:17.435
DM: I tried not to look.

02:55:17.435 --> 02:55:18.515
DM: The smell was...

02:55:21.255 --> 02:55:23.835
DM: I don't know if you've ever smelled burning flesh.

02:55:26.715 --> 02:55:27.715
DM: It's overwhelming.

02:55:29.015 --> 02:55:29.775
Seeker: Hmm. That it is.

02:55:32.255 --> 02:55:33.655
DM: Don't know how I got on the boat,

02:55:35.975 --> 02:55:36.035
DM: but-

02:55:36.055 --> 02:55:37.475
Seeker: Was anyone up at the temple at the time?

02:55:38.795 --> 02:55:39.415
DM: There were

02:55:40.755 --> 02:55:41.915
DM: 10 pilgrims and

02:55:43.435 --> 02:55:45.395
Unknown: some priests. There's always priests.

02:55:46.575 --> 02:55:47.895
Seeker: And they stay up there in the night?

02:55:48.795 --> 02:55:54.155
DM: The priests, th- there's a, they have some, some houses up there.

02:55:54.155 --> 02:55:55.075
Seeker: In the caldera?

02:55:55.075 --> 02:55:56.475
DM: In the caldera.

02:55:56.475 --> 02:56:01.787
DM: There's a small priests'- A community there.

02:56:02.527 --> 02:56:06.107
DM: Pilgrims often will [coughs] spend the night in prayer

02:56:07.707 --> 02:56:08.547
DM: on the path up,

02:56:09.467 --> 02:56:11.467
DM: the path from the temple to the waters.

02:56:12.707 --> 02:56:16.367
DM: It is said to draw Kaikkea's attention, those who are

02:56:17.487 --> 02:56:18.787
DM: willing to endure the

02:56:20.067 --> 02:56:21.487
DM: long night. It's,

02:56:22.407 --> 02:56:24.787
DM: even in the depths of winter, it's rarely cold-

02:56:25.207 --> 02:56:25.327
Unknown: Hmm

02:56:25.327 --> 02:56:33.387
Unknown: ... from the warmth of the volcano.

02:56:33.387 --> 02:56:36.827
DM: Many also stay at the longhouse, of course, where I was sleeping.

02:56:42.367 --> 02:56:43.667
Seeker: What do you think explains all this?

02:56:46.287 --> 02:56:46.967
DM: A demon.

02:56:50.347 --> 02:56:52.187
DM: People say it was Urgall the Black,

02:56:53.467 --> 02:56:53.907
DM: that he

02:56:56.607 --> 02:56:57.607
DM: traded his...

02:57:00.327 --> 02:57:01.767
DM: made some pact with

02:57:04.187 --> 02:57:06.387
DM: a demon lord for, for power.

02:57:07.787 --> 02:57:07.967
Unknown: Hmm.

02:57:09.967 --> 02:57:11.327
DM: And he is who attacked.

02:57:13.447 --> 02:57:17.047
Seeker: But why would he do, why would he attack this sacred island?

02:57:18.487 --> 02:57:20.367
DM: Maybe his master commanded it.

02:57:26.407 --> 02:57:28.127
Unknown: How long ago was this again?

02:57:28.127 --> 02:57:33.207
DM: It was, like, in the spring. It was, like, seven months ago, eight months ago or something.

02:57:33.807 --> 02:57:35.087
Seeker: Has anything changed since then?

02:57:37.147 --> 02:57:39.347
DM: More people have gone and died.

02:57:39.347 --> 02:57:39.867
Seeker: That's all.

02:57:42.907 --> 02:57:45.587
DM: Three months ago, we stopped trying.

02:57:46.787 --> 02:57:46.947
Unknown: Hmm.

02:57:46.947 --> 02:57:48.127
DM: 'Cause our

02:57:49.807 --> 02:57:51.107
DM: strongest warriors,

02:57:52.887 --> 02:57:53.807
DM: those who could be

02:57:55.087 --> 02:57:55.507
DM: sent,

02:57:56.907 --> 02:58:00.787
DM: had died, or not returned, in any case.

02:58:01.487 --> 02:58:11.427
Seeker: Those in Tollen have told us about this, that are removed from the whole situation, spoke of it as an island that had been cursed, but now we understand, I think, that the curse actually is

02:58:12.747 --> 02:58:18.187
Seeker: the sacred protection of the island against those who would set foot on it unblessed.

02:58:18.847 --> 02:58:19.787
DM: Yes.

02:58:19.787 --> 02:58:20.607
Seeker: And what

02:58:22.267 --> 02:58:24.087
Seeker: form exactly does that protection take?

02:58:24.087 --> 02:58:26.607
Seeker: Does it simply prevent them from leaving?

02:58:29.487 --> 02:58:31.447
Unknown: If I knew, I could tell you, maybe.

02:58:31.487 --> 02:58:31.747
DM: Hmm.

02:58:33.807 --> 02:58:35.747
Seeker: We saw what appeared to be a ring of fog.

02:58:37.147 --> 02:58:38.827
Delwath: Are you familiar with the shoreline of the island?

02:58:40.467 --> 02:58:43.087
DM: Very, yes. I've sailed around it several times.

02:58:46.187 --> 02:58:46.707
Delwath: Are there any

02:58:50.987 --> 02:58:52.267
Delwath: hidden coves,

02:58:54.907 --> 02:58:58.867
Delwath: spots where we could approach from a different direction?

02:58:59.907 --> 02:59:01.367
DM: How good at climbing are you?

02:59:02.387 --> 02:59:02.927
Wellby: Excellent.

02:59:04.347 --> 02:59:08.127
DM: Yes. There is a cove on the north side,

02:59:10.147 --> 02:59:11.847
DM: so it's sort of below the caldera,

02:59:14.267 --> 02:59:15.507
DM: but it requires about a

02:59:17.087 --> 02:59:20.487
DM: 30, 40-foot climb up a steep cliff.

02:59:24.107 --> 02:59:25.147
DM: It's a safe harbor, though,

02:59:26.787 --> 02:59:27.847
DM: if you wish to sail there.

02:59:28.367 --> 02:59:31.607
Seeker: And that would emerge into the caldera on the temple side or the open side?

02:59:32.367 --> 02:59:34.667
DM: No, it would emerge... It doesn't emerge into the caldera.

02:59:34.667 --> 02:59:35.547
DM: It emerges on the,

02:59:36.687 --> 02:59:41.507
DM: like, outside the wall of the caldera, but on the opposite side from where the entrance is.

02:59:41.607 --> 02:59:42.447
Wellby: I see. Yeah.

02:59:42.447 --> 02:59:47.387
DM: So, you'd have to then, if you wanted to get into the caldera, you'd have to climb over the caldera wall as well,

02:59:49.007 --> 02:59:52.087
DM: which was tough. I mean, it's a steep caldera cone, right?

02:59:52.087 --> 02:59:52.787
DM: Like, so it was like-

02:59:54.327 --> 02:59:54.347
Unknown: Yeah

02:59:54.347 --> 02:59:57.827
DM: ... it's a climb, but not, like, you know, impossible, right?

02:59:57.827 --> 02:59:58.627
DM: Like, it's just...

02:59:59.407 --> 03:00:02.447
DM: It's not like a sheer... It's not like 1,000-foot sheer cliff or anything.

03:00:02.447 --> 03:00:04.447
DM: It's like a steep hike, basically.

03:00:05.627 --> 03:00:11.787
DM: Jagged rocks, not, like, easiest terrain, but, like, not really that much of a challenge for...

03:00:15.167 --> 03:00:19.467
DM: It's definitely climbable. Like, you could see from the air, the, the caldera wall is definitely climbable.

03:00:20.327 --> 03:00:22.747
DM: It might not be easy, but it's definitely climbable.

03:00:22.747 --> 03:00:26.427
Seeker: Don't know if you saw us come in, but we actually have a flying ship.

03:00:28.427 --> 03:00:29.967
DM: I had heard some rumors and

03:00:31.147 --> 03:00:33.107
Unknown: did not believe that.

03:00:34.787 --> 03:00:35.807
Seeker: Great ship of stone.

03:00:36.707 --> 03:00:37.607
Seeker: This may give us some

03:00:39.147 --> 03:00:41.347
Seeker: non-obvious options for the approach.

03:00:43.407 --> 03:00:49.847
Delwath: Well, we may want an element of surprise, which is hard to achieve if we fly in with a giant ship of stone.

03:00:50.147 --> 03:00:51.167
Wellby: We'd already fly over it?

03:00:52.027 --> 03:00:52.767
Delwath: Yeah, but we're pretty high up.

03:00:52.767 --> 03:00:53.827
DM: You are pretty high up.

03:00:54.567 --> 03:00:55.227
Wellby: Okay.

03:00:55.247 --> 03:00:55.707
DM: And there's-

03:00:55.707 --> 03:00:55.767
Unknown: I mean-

03:00:55.767 --> 03:00:58.187
DM: ... enough cloud cover that you wouldn't be obvious

03:00:58.187 --> 03:00:58.187
Unknown: ...

03:00:59.787 --> 03:01:03.567
Delwath: also, just because they know we're in the area doesn't necessarily know-

03:01:03.607 --> 03:01:04.067
DM: Right

03:01:04.067 --> 03:01:05.807
Delwath: ... exactly where or when we're coming from.

03:01:06.267 --> 03:01:07.947
Delwath: I mean, not to say we shouldn't fly across.

03:01:07.967 --> 03:01:10.867
Delwath: I wouldn't wanna try to go through the mist on a boat.

03:01:10.867 --> 03:01:12.827
Unknown: That seems like it's ended badly.

03:01:13.207 --> 03:01:15.447
Wellby: Is there any other way through the caldera

03:01:16.507 --> 03:01:18.447
Wellby: besides the passageway?

03:01:18.587 --> 03:01:20.207
DM: Other than scaling the walls?

03:01:20.287 --> 03:01:21.527
Wellby: Mm-hmm.

03:01:21.547 --> 03:01:22.767
Delwath: How about inside the caldera?

03:01:22.767 --> 03:01:23.307
Delwath: Is there any

03:01:24.907 --> 03:01:25.807
Delwath: hidden spots,

03:01:27.267 --> 03:01:28.187
Delwath: small caves,

03:01:30.667 --> 03:01:33.967
Unknown: particularly well-placed rocks to hide behind?

03:01:35.387 --> 03:01:37.687
Delwath: What would be the best path if we were trying to, uh,

03:01:38.987 --> 03:01:40.347
Delwath: get to the, say, the temple

03:01:41.667 --> 03:01:42.687
Delwath: and or the, um,

03:01:43.847 --> 03:01:48.567
Delwath: the, the temple complex or the entrance unseen?

03:01:49.967 --> 03:01:52.227
DM: Jorma once told me that there are

03:01:53.767 --> 03:01:56.027
DM: lava tubes that run under the-

03:01:58.347 --> 03:01:59.287
Delwath: Ooh, I like that idea

03:01:59.287 --> 03:02:00.867
DM: ... run under the springs.

03:02:01.487 --> 03:02:06.855
DM: In fact, that's what feeds the Waters of healing and the waters of

03:02:08.335 --> 03:02:11.635
DM: communion from the sacred spring itself

03:02:11.635 --> 03:02:12.755
Seeker: The lava tubes

03:02:14.275 --> 03:02:15.875
Unknown: Don't know where one would enter them

03:02:17.475 --> 03:02:18.155
Seeker: Who told you this?

03:02:19.615 --> 03:02:21.635
DM: Jorma. He, I guess, was...

03:02:21.635 --> 03:02:22.135
DM: Now I'm

03:02:23.955 --> 03:02:25.255
DM: sure he's dead. He was the

03:02:26.315 --> 03:02:26.795
DM: priest,

03:02:28.555 --> 03:02:30.275
DM: the, the priest of the waters.

03:02:32.615 --> 03:02:34.395
DM: Head priest of the island.

03:02:34.395 --> 03:02:35.535
Delwath: Would anybody, um,

03:02:37.054 --> 03:02:39.775
Unknown: know more about these types of things?

03:02:39.775 --> 03:02:43.175
Unknown: Any, anyone who's been on pilgrimage several times?

03:02:45.175 --> 03:02:46.255
Delwath: Might have spent time,

03:02:47.975 --> 03:02:49.595
Delwath: been close to the priests?

03:02:49.595 --> 03:02:57.055
DM: [coughs] I think I have traveled to the island more than anybody else here.

03:02:58.355 --> 03:02:59.115
DM: There's not a lot of

03:03:00.735 --> 03:03:02.155
DM: pilgrims these days.

03:03:04.135 --> 03:03:05.015
DM: Word has gotten out.

03:03:08.455 --> 03:03:08.635
Unknown: But

03:03:09.655 --> 03:03:12.935
Unknown: in the past, there must have been substant- many pilgrims.

03:03:14.455 --> 03:03:14.835
DM: Yes.

03:03:24.595 --> 03:03:26.615
Wellby: All right, so we can hidden cove and climb up.

03:03:28.355 --> 03:03:32.395
Wellby: We can try and get through the lava tubes, but we don't know how to get into them.

03:03:34.575 --> 03:03:34.815
DM: Which one-

03:03:34.875 --> 03:03:36.635
Seeker: Interesting question. How would you, how would you find

03:03:37.655 --> 03:03:38.515
Seeker: a lava tube

03:03:40.515 --> 03:03:43.015
Seeker: exit hole from the side of the caldera?

03:03:43.015 --> 03:03:45.495
Seeker: Is that even a thing? Like, what, how do lava tubes form?

03:03:46.935 --> 03:03:50.215
Seeker: I used my geology knowledge to predict lava tube shapes.

03:03:50.255 --> 03:03:53.295
DM: I mean, it's, like, it's entirely possible that the wa- exit...

03:03:54.415 --> 03:03:56.375
DM: So I mean, lava tubes form when-

03:03:56.375 --> 03:03:57.115
Seeker: Excuse me one second

03:03:57.115 --> 03:03:58.535
DM: ... flowing lava cools.

03:03:58.915 --> 03:03:59.455
Wellby: Right

03:03:59.455 --> 03:04:00.195
DM: Right? And the lava

03:04:01.275 --> 03:04:04.535
DM: cools around it, and the hot lava in the middle keeps flowing.

03:04:04.555 --> 03:04:04.975
Seeker: Mm-hmm.

03:04:06.415 --> 03:04:07.915
DM: But they often collapse.

03:04:08.275 --> 03:04:08.655
Seeker: Right.

03:04:09.215 --> 03:04:15.794
DM: So I mean, it's entirely possible that if you walk around the back side of the caldera, you would just see, like, a big open cave.

03:04:15.794 --> 03:04:16.155
Kenzo: Right.

03:04:17.115 --> 03:04:20.555
DM: But you also might have to look for, like, a

03:04:22.135 --> 03:04:29.915
DM: section of collapsed rock that could be excavated to lead into a tunnel or something.

03:04:29.915 --> 03:04:38.795
DM: But I mean, they, it's, they would be probably f- usually fairly wide diameter, like, you know, 10 feet.

03:04:38.795 --> 03:04:42.655
DM: So it's not like you're looking for a tiny little hole, like you'd have to excavate to...

03:04:46.155 --> 03:04:54.015
DM: And given, you know, the t- topography, like figuring out where lava might have flowed is not

03:04:55.175 --> 03:04:55.975
DM: impossible.

03:04:56.655 --> 03:04:56.915
Wellby: Mm-hmm.

03:04:57.615 --> 03:05:00.255
DM: But it, you know, it's a search, right?

03:05:00.255 --> 03:05:00.475
DM: You don't-

03:05:01.675 --> 03:05:01.835
Unknown: Right

03:05:01.835 --> 03:05:04.135
DM: ... you have some idea of what you'd be looking for,

03:05:05.355 --> 03:05:09.635
DM: but how long it takes to find it or whether you even could is sort of hard to know.

03:05:09.715 --> 03:05:10.055
Seeker: Mm-hmm.

03:05:13.035 --> 03:05:18.815
Seeker: I wonder if there are any creatures that would inhabit lava tubes, like if there'd be some- somebody we could ask, like a

03:05:20.655 --> 03:05:21.755
Seeker: bird would nest there.

03:05:23.275 --> 03:05:25.455
Delwath: They've all, I think the wildlife has all-

03:05:25.455 --> 03:05:30.935
DM: I mean, lizards and things, but as far as you're aware from Kenzo is there's no wildlife left behind.

03:05:30.935 --> 03:05:32.635
Unknown: Ah, right, right. Crazy.

03:05:33.955 --> 03:05:37.915
Seeker: It's also a pretty c- chilly climate, so there wouldn't be lizards necessarily.

03:05:38.135 --> 03:05:40.695
DM: Right. Lizards would be in tropical lava tubes.

03:05:40.695 --> 03:05:43.555
DM: I was thinking about Hawaii, but this is not Hawaii.

03:05:43.675 --> 03:05:43.915
Unknown: Yeah.

03:05:45.235 --> 03:05:45.555
Wellby: Right.

03:05:46.635 --> 03:05:47.735
Seeker: Probably mostly be birds.

03:05:48.195 --> 03:05:48.455
DM: Right.

03:05:50.055 --> 03:05:52.815
DM: But most seabirds wouldn't nest in caves.

03:05:54.895 --> 03:05:56.015
DM: They nest on cliffs.

03:05:56.055 --> 03:05:56.395
Delwath: Right.

03:05:57.455 --> 03:05:59.875
Delwath: Yeah, they wouldn't nest inside. They'd n- they'd nest on the cliff.

03:06:00.275 --> 03:06:00.275
DM: Right.

03:06:00.275 --> 03:06:00.975
Delwath: Would they? Right.

03:06:02.115 --> 03:06:03.595
Delwath: Or above or around.

03:06:03.595 --> 03:06:06.455
Delwath: They would know potentially because they would be familiar with the cliffs.

03:06:06.455 --> 03:06:07.075
DM: Yes. Yes, yes, yes.

03:06:07.075 --> 03:06:08.715
Delwath: But they're all dead, so I'm not sure that helps us.

03:06:08.715 --> 03:06:09.155
DM: But they're all dead, so that's

03:06:12.935 --> 03:06:12.995
DM: no.

03:06:14.175 --> 03:06:15.095
Wellby: [sighs] Should we, uh...

03:06:17.235 --> 03:06:18.775
Delwath: I think we...

03:06:18.775 --> 03:06:20.695
Unknown: I, I don't think there's much more to learn, right?

03:06:20.695 --> 03:06:21.015
Wellby: Agreed.

03:06:22.815 --> 03:06:31.355
DM: Sort of bows and, and backs off and says, "I wish Kaikkea's blessing on you."

03:06:34.615 --> 03:06:37.255
Seeker: What did we learn, sorry, what did we learn about this guy's sort of profession?

03:06:37.615 --> 03:06:37.755
Unknown: He's a ferry-

03:06:37.775 --> 03:06:39.175
DM: He was, like, the ferry. He was, like, a

03:06:40.555 --> 03:06:43.355
DM: f- ferry captain, basically. He sailed people to the island.

03:06:43.355 --> 03:06:43.415
Unknown: Ah.

03:06:43.655 --> 03:06:45.495
DM: He, he was, like, the person who would take pilgrims.

03:06:45.855 --> 03:06:48.095
Seeker: So I was just thinking, should we invite him to go along with us?

03:06:48.675 --> 03:06:48.695
DM: Oh.

03:06:48.695 --> 03:06:49.175
Seeker: But if he's-

03:06:49.195 --> 03:06:49.795
Wellby: What?

03:06:49.795 --> 03:06:51.895
Seeker: He's kind of standard human, he would just get squashed.

03:06:52.455 --> 03:06:52.915
DM: And I'd-

03:06:52.935 --> 03:06:54.295
Seeker: He would die. He would vaporize instantly.

03:06:54.295 --> 03:06:56.055
Delwath: But also he sounded terrified.

03:06:56.075 --> 03:06:57.115
Delwath: I don't think he would accept.

03:06:57.195 --> 03:06:57.375
Unknown: Yeah.

03:06:57.375 --> 03:06:57.635
DM: Right.

03:06:58.055 --> 03:06:59.515
Seeker: It's just he knows the place, so.

03:06:59.915 --> 03:07:01.535
DM: He definitely would not accept.

03:07:01.535 --> 03:07:03.575
DM: [laughs]

03:07:03.635 --> 03:07:06.495
Delwath: All right. Should we keep and go on then?

03:07:06.735 --> 03:07:08.795
DM: I mean, he's, like, he drew a map, right?

03:07:08.795 --> 03:07:08.855
Unknown: Yeah.

03:07:08.855 --> 03:07:10.455
Unknown: It's a Wellby copy map.

03:07:10.455 --> 03:07:12.575
DM: So you, you know, you do have that perspective.

03:07:12.595 --> 03:07:12.895
Delwath: Yes.

03:07:12.975 --> 03:07:15.755
Delwath: I'm gonna assume that he actually explained things in quite a bit of detail.

03:07:15.795 --> 03:07:16.935
DM: Yeah, yeah, yeah. There's no-

03:07:17.035 --> 03:07:19.995
Delwath: Such that when it comes up that you need to know something about the layout-

03:07:19.995 --> 03:07:20.375
DM: You would know

03:07:20.375 --> 03:07:21.335
Delwath: ... he can just tell us.

03:07:21.395 --> 03:07:21.715
DM: Yeah, yeah.

03:07:22.575 --> 03:07:22.715
DM: Of course.

03:07:22.735 --> 03:07:26.175
Delwath: Without you having to actually make up every single nook and cranny right now.

03:07:26.235 --> 03:07:26.675
DM: Right.

03:07:27.915 --> 03:07:33.655
Kenzo: So in summary, there's a pirate who's in the volcano.

03:07:34.335 --> 03:07:35.195
Delwath: The pirate...

03:07:37.015 --> 03:07:42.055
Delwath: The, okay, so the, the caldera, there's a, there's a dock with a pirate ship.

03:07:42.155 --> 03:07:42.515
Delwath: Uh-huh.

03:07:42.555 --> 03:07:44.035
Delwath: A road that goes to the

03:07:44.835 --> 03:07:45.355
Delwath: temple entrance.

03:07:45.355 --> 03:07:47.875
Kenzo: But I mean in terms of the plot, like the, the, there's a pirate-

03:07:48.535 --> 03:07:48.615
Unknown: Yeah

03:07:48.615 --> 03:07:49.755
Kenzo: ... who is in the volcano.

03:07:50.495 --> 03:07:52.235
Unknown: Well, no, we don't know where the pirate is.

03:07:53.035 --> 03:07:53.155
Unknown: Well-

03:07:53.295 --> 03:07:54.035
DM: He's somewhere on the Skaer Islands.

03:07:54.055 --> 03:07:54.795
Kenzo: But what's the curse?

03:07:55.175 --> 03:07:56.415
Wellby: He made a deal with a demon.

03:07:56.655 --> 03:07:59.475
Unknown: He made a deal with a demon, and the demon maybe sent him.

03:07:59.495 --> 03:08:00.735
Delwath: I don't know.

03:08:00.735 --> 03:08:02.535
Wellby: But he and 50 of his crew landed,

03:08:03.595 --> 03:08:04.635
Wellby: burned everything down.

03:08:05.295 --> 03:08:06.755
